// axis-bridge.axreg — Core IR 0.5 registry for AI2 bridge functions.
//
// Identities: §5b bootstrap rule — SHA-256 of the UTF-8 function name bytes.
// Types: declared as (Unit) → Unit for all bridge functions.
// The verifier is bypassed (--no-verify) during AI2 compilation because
// bridge functions are polymorphic and cannot be fully typed in the 0.5
// type system. This registry exists solely for name → identity resolution
// during Core IR 0.5 lowering.

registry axis-bridge 0.5

// TYPES: axis-types.axreg owns every type this file's fns reference —
// Unit, Int, Text, Bool, Value, TextList, ValueList, Bytes, Fn, Dec, Float.
// They were declared here until AXREG_LAYERING_VALUE_OWNERSHIP_V1 was
// actually enforced in the files; declaring any of them here again is a
// hard load error (duplicate type name), not a shadow or an override.
// The definitions moved verbatim, so every signature below is unchanged.
// seq(a, b) -> b. Compiler-injected (nf_lowering) to thread a discarded
// side-effecting `let _ = eff(); tail` inside an `if` arm into the arm's result,
// so branch-scoping keeps the effect inside that arm. First arg evaluated for
// ordering only; second returned. Declared fullIo so each injected seq is a
// distinct (non-CSE'd) node. Identity = sha256("seq").
// `variadic true` is NOT about argument COUNT here — seq always gets exactly
// two, by construction in seq_scope_arm_effects, and with a two-entry `in` the
// verifier still ENFORCES exactly two (validation/core_ir.rs step 2a). It is the
// only way this grammar can say "polymorphic in both positions": variadic is
// what skips the per-argument type check, and without it `seq(eff, result)`
// rejects any result whose type has no single-payload arm in `Value` — every
// ValueList and Tuple, and before the AsDec/AsFloat/AsBytes arms landed, those
// too.
//
// `in (Value, Value)` / `out Value` therefore fix the COUNT but are otherwise
// documentation of the shape, not the checked contract. The real result type
// is the type of args[1]: the verifier special-cases this identity (validation/core_ir.rs, step 5) because
// a monomorphic `out` would pin every seq-wrapped `if` arm to `Value` and
// diverge from its sibling arm for every result type but one. Measured before
// that rule existed:
//   CIfBranchDivergence { then_type: 23945287…(Value), else_type: ffff3528…(Text) }
// on `if c { let a = io_println(msg) Text("yes") } else { Text("no") }`.
fn seq
  identity 0x4f5260d71c7e332a2c94bf0b36ce79c9a33ba6f037535665c15fa5bc8a2fccfc
  kind     leaf
  deterministic true
  idempotent    false
  in (Value, Value)
  out Value
  effect fullIo
  variadic true
end
