// ── Tuple / Constructor (tuple.rs) ────────────────────────────────────────────
// THREE PRODUCT FAMILIES, TWO STRICT ACCESSORS, ONE SPANNING ACCESSOR.
// tuple_field and ctor_field are declared identically — in (Value, Int) out
// Value — so nothing here distinguishes them and crossing them is a PROCESS
// ABORT, not a verify error:
//
//   value_make(a, b, …)             -> Value::Ctor   read: ctor_field
//   list_of_3 / list_cons / list_nil-> Value::List    read: tuple_field, list_get
//   tcp_listen / tcp_listen_shared  -> Value::Tuple   read: tuple_field
//
//   tuple_field: expected Tuple or List, got Ctor { tag: 0, fields: [...] }
//   (use ctor_field for a Value(..)(..)-constructed Ctor)
//
// value_0/value_1/value_2 read ALL THREE and never panic (runtime/tuple.rs
// `value_field`), which makes them the right default when the family is not
// obvious at the call site. Their price: they stop at index 2 and return
// Value::Unit SILENTLY for an out-of-range index — a quiet wrong answer
// instead of a loud panic. Use the strict accessor when you know the
// constructor and want the index checked by a crash.
//
// The names mislead: `value_make` builds a Ctor, and `tuple_field` reads a
// List as happily as a Tuple.
fn tuple_field
  identity 0xcd53821c1e662647c6170a5949859816cf739b162bf4629743de046154e6fd68
